  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1224 PM CDT Tue Oct 13 2026\n\nA cold front currently sits along Highway 50 and and is slowly \nexpected to drift southward as the afternoon progresses. South of \nthis front expect continued above normal temperatures and southwest \nwinds with increased low level cloudiness and north to northeast \nwinds north of this front. This will create a wide range of \ntemperatures for highs this afternoon ranging from upper 60s to low \n70s north of the front with low 90s south of the front. \n\nModels are still on track to have strong to severe thunderstorms \ndevelop this afternoon mainly east of Highway 283 and south of \nHighway 50. SPC currently has a slight risk of severe storms east of \na line from Liberal to Dodge City, to Great Bend. Storms in this \narea will have the potential to produce large hail and damaging \nwinds. An isolated tornado will also be possible across south \ncentral Kansas. Precipitation chances remain high east of Highway 83 \nto night spreading across the remainder of the CWA Wednesday. The \nbest chance of precipitation looks to be Wednesday night as another \ndisturbance moves through the area. Precipitation chances then come \nto an end Thursday.  The remainder of the forecast is expected to \nremain dry as upper level ridging builds across the western United \nStates with high pressure becoming the dominate feature across the \nCentral Plains. Cooler weather is also expected Wednesday through \nthe end of the forecast with highs in the 60s to low 70s Wednesday \nand Thursday, then mid 50s to around 60 degrees Friday. We then \nrebound into the 60s Saturday with 70s Sunday and Monday. Lows in \nthe 30s are expected Saturday morning and may reach freezing across \nportions of west central Kansas.",
